From 066e4b17d1392637d4bba0fee6fb96b3a591e07b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: soyjxck <soyjxck@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 01:36:32 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 06/13] cellPad: SDL2 on Windows too (PS3RECOMP_PAD_SDL2), pads
 by instance id

Windows read XInput only, so a DualSense, DualShock 4 or Switch Pro pad
-- HID devices XInput never enumerates -- was an empty slot unless Steam
Input or DS4Windows stood in. PS3RECOMP_PAD_SDL2=ON builds the SDL2
backend on Windows as well (SDL2 from vcpkg; audio stays on WASAPI); the
keyboard fallback no longer lives inside the XInput block.

The SDL2 backend itself:

- Slots are matched to pads by instance id. By device index, a pad's
  index shifts down when one before it is unplugged, and
  SDL_GameControllerOpen on an already-open device returns the same
  object: slot 0 reopened slot 1's pad after player 1 unplugged, and both
  ports read it.
- Hints before the subsystem starts: background events allowed (the pad
  is read whether or not an SDL window has the focus, as XInput does),
  buttons by position (the bottom face button is CROSS on a Switch Pro
  pad too), PS4/PS5 rumble over Bluetooth, and on Windows SDL's own
  joystick thread, which owns the device-notification window and
  re-checks the XInput slots: there is no SDL window here and the pad is
  polled from whichever guest thread asks.
- Joystick and controller events ignored: the state is polled and nobody
  drains SDL's queue.
- A centred stick reads 128, not 127.
- A log line per pad connected or disconnected.

Tested on Windows (Drakengard 3): Xbox One, Xbox Series X and DualSense
pads plugged and unplugged mid-game, each taking the port a PS3 would
give it; the DualSense drives the game from port 0.

Ported to SARPBC-recomp (T008) from soyjxck/ps3recomp 7e52cef1e6f5 (MIT),
libs/input part only; the original author is kept.
---
 libs/input/cellPad.c | 260 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++-----------------
 1 file changed, 158 insertions(+), 102 deletions(-)

diff --git a/libs/input/cellPad.c b/libs/input/cellPad.c
index 78187d9..3747068 100644
--- a/libs/input/cellPad.c
+++ b/libs/input/cellPad.c
@@ -4,10 +4,12 @@
  * Reads real gamepad input from the host and translates to PS3 pad format.
  *
  * Backend selection:
- *   - Windows default: XInput (no extra dependencies)
+ *   - Windows default: XInput (no extra dependencies; Xbox-type pads only)
  *   - Everywhere else / if PS3RECOMP_PAD_USE_SDL2 is defined: SDL2 GameController
  *
- * Define PS3RECOMP_PAD_USE_SDL2 to force SDL2 backend on Windows.
+ * Define PS3RECOMP_PAD_USE_SDL2 (CMake: PS3RECOMP_PAD_SDL2=ON) for SDL2 on
+ * Windows as well: DualShock 4, DualSense, Switch Pro and the other pads
+ * XInput never sees, wired or over Bluetooth.
  */
 
 #include <time.h>
@@ -159,84 +161,6 @@ static u8 pad_xinput_stick_to_u8(short raw, short deadzone)
     return (u8)val;
 }
 
-/* Keyboard fallback for port 0.
- *
- * The only backend here is XInput, so on a machine with no controller plugged
- * in a title gets a pad that is reported present and never presses anything --
- * it can be watched but not played. That is what The Simpsons Arcade Game hit:
- * it reached its attract loop and no input existed to start a game.
- *
- * Only fills in for port 0, only when XInput found nothing there, so a real
- * controller always wins and nothing changes for a port that has one. Keys are
- * read only while a window of THIS process is in the foreground, so typing in
- * another application does not drive the game. Set PAD_NO_KEYBOARD=1 to
- * disable it entirely.
- *
- * Arrows = d-pad, Z/X/A/S = cross/circle/square/triangle, Q/W = L1/R1,
- * 1/2 = L2/R2, Enter = START, Tab = SELECT. The left stick mirrors the d-pad
